Spring Config Server TLS / Certificate Architecture

1. Overview

The application server communicates with an existing Spring Config Server over HTTPS using mutual TLS (mTLS).

The application server also exposes its own HTTPS API endpoint.

Therefore, the application participates in two different TLS relationships:

                         App Server
                             │
              ┌──────────────┴──────────────┐
              │                             │
              ▼                             ▼
       Config Server mTLS              App API HTTPS
       (App = Client)                  (App = Server)
              │                             │
              ▼                             ▼
        Config Server                  API Consumers

These two TLS relationships use certificates for different purposes.

⸻

2. Files on the App Server

Example:

/app/webAS/config/ssl/
├── cacerts
├── webbroker.delta.td.com.jks
├── wtinv-api.dev.td.com.crt.pem
└── wtinv-api.dev.td.com.key.pem

Conceptually:

App Server
│
├── webbroker.delta.td.com.jks
│      └── Client identity used for Config Server mTLS
│
├── cacerts
│      └── Trusted CA certificates
│
├── wtinv-api.dev.td.com.crt.pem
│      └── App API server certificate
│
└── wtinv-api.dev.td.com.key.pem
       └── Private key for App API server certificate

⸻

3. JKS — App Identity for Config Server

Example:

webbroker.delta.td.com.jks

JKS stands for Java KeyStore.

The JKS used as a keystore normally contains a PrivateKeyEntry:

webbroker.delta.td.com.jks
│
└── PrivateKeyEntry
      │
      ├── Client Certificate
      │
      ├── Private Key
      │
      └── Certificate Chain
            ├── Intermediate CA
            └── Root CA

The important concept is:

Keystore = "Who am I?"

When the App Server connects to the Config Server using mTLS, it uses the certificate and private key stored in this JKS to prove its identity.

The Spring configuration may look like:

spring.cloud.config.tls.enabled=true
spring.cloud.config.tls.key-store=${server.ssl.key-store}
spring.cloud.config.tls.key-store-type=${server.ssl.key-store-type}
spring.cloud.config.tls.key-store-password=${server.ssl.key-store-password}
spring.cloud.config.tls.key-password=${server.ssl.key-password}

The keystore can therefore be used by the Spring Config Client during the TLS handshake.

⸻

4. cacerts — Truststore

Example:

/app/webAS/config/ssl/cacerts

The truststore contains certificates representing Certificate Authorities that the application trusts.

For example:

cacerts
│
├── Corporate Root CA
├── Corporate Intermediate CA
├── Other Trusted Root CA
└── Other Trusted Intermediate CA

The important concept is:

Truststore = "Who do I trust?"

When the Config Server sends its server certificate, the App Server verifies the certificate against the trusted CAs in cacerts.

The Spring configuration may look like:

spring.cloud.config.tls.trust-store=file:/app/webAS/config/ssl/cacerts
spring.cloud.config.tls.trust-store-type=jks
spring.cloud.config.tls.trust-store-password=${SPRING_TLS_TRUSTSTORE_PASSWORD}

⸻

5. Config Server Certificate Validation

Suppose the application connects to:

https://webbroker.config.pat.td.com:19988

The Config Server has its own server certificate:

Config Server Certificate
│
├── Subject / SAN
│      └── webbroker.config.pat.td.com
│
├── Public Key
│
├── Issuer
│      └── Corporate Intermediate CA
│
└── Validity

During the TLS handshake, the Config Server sends this certificate to the App Server.

The App Server then validates it:

Config Server Certificate
          │
          │ Issued by
          ▼
Corporate Intermediate CA
          │
          │ Issued by
          ▼
Corporate Root CA
          │
          ▼
 App Server cacerts
          │
          └── Trusted

The App Server normally does not need the Config Server leaf certificate directly in cacerts.

It normally needs to trust the CA that issued the Config Server certificate.

⸻

6. Client Certificate Validation

Because the Config Server requires mTLS, authentication also happens in the opposite direction.

The Config Server asks the application:

"Who are you?"

The App Server responds using the client certificate stored in its JKS.

App Server
    │
    │ Client Certificate
    │ + proof of Private Key
    ▼
Config Server
    │
    │ Validate certificate
    ▼
Trusted Client

Therefore:

App JKS
   │
   └── Proves App identity
             │
             ▼
       Config Server
App cacerts
   │
   └── Validates Config Server identity

⸻

7. Complete mTLS Handshake

The complete flow looks like this:

App Server                               Config Server
    │                                         │
    │ ----------- Client Hello -------------> │
    │                                         │
    │ <--------- Server Certificate --------- │
    │                                         │
    │ Validate Config Server certificate      │
    │ using App truststore (cacerts)          │
    │                                         │
    │                 OK                      │
    │                                         │
    │ <------- Request Client Certificate --- │
    │                                         │
    │ Read client identity from JKS           │
    │                                         │
    │ -------- Client Certificate ----------> │
    │ -------- Private Key Proof -----------> │
    │                                         │
    │                           Validate App   │
    │                           Certificate   │
    │                                │        │
    │                                OK       │
    │                                         │
    │ ========== mTLS Established =========== │
    │                                         │
    │ ------ Request Spring Config ---------> │
    │                                         │
    │ <---------- Configuration ------------- │

Both sides therefore authenticate each other.

⸻

8. PEM Certificate and Private Key

The App Server also contains:

wtinv-api.dev.td.com.crt.pem
wtinv-api.dev.td.com.key.pem

These are used when the application itself acts as an HTTPS server.

API Consumer
     │
     │ HTTPS
     ▼
WTINV App Server
     │
     ├── wtinv-api.dev.td.com.crt.pem
     └── wtinv-api.dev.td.com.key.pem

The two files have different purposes:

.crt.pem
   │
   └── Public Certificate
       "I am wtinv-api.dev.td.com"
.key.pem
   │
   └── Private Key
       Proves ownership of the certificate

The private key must remain secret.

⸻

9. Why Does the App Need Both JKS and PEM Files?

Because the application has two different TLS roles.

                         App Server
                             │
          ┌──────────────────┴──────────────────┐
          │                                     │
          ▼                                     ▼
     TLS Client                             TLS Server
          │                                     │
          │                                     │
Connect to Config Server                Serve App API
          │                                     │
          ▼                                     ▼
        JKS                              CRT.PEM + KEY.PEM
          │                                     │
 Client Certificate                     Server Certificate
 + Private Key                           + Private Key

For the Config Server connection:

App Server = Client
Config Server = Server

For the App API:

API Consumer = Client
App Server    = Server

Therefore, these certificates serve different purposes.

⸻

10. Keystore Password vs Key Password

These are two different concepts.

A JKS may look like:

app.jks
│
│  Protected by:
│  KEYSTORE PASSWORD
│
└── PrivateKeyEntry
      │
      ├── Certificate
      │
      └── Private Key
             │
             └── Protected by:
                 KEY PASSWORD

Therefore:

KEYSTORE PASSWORD
        │
        └── Opens/accesses the keystore
KEY PASSWORD
        │
        └── Accesses the private key

They can have different values.

However, many Java applications configure them with the same value for simplicity.

For example:

SERVER_SSL_KEY_STORE_PASSWORD = password-A
SERVER_SSL_KEY_PASSWORD       = password-A

Conceptually they are still different passwords.

⸻

11. Truststore Password

The truststore has its own password:

cacerts
│
│  TRUSTSTORE PASSWORD
│
├── Root CA
├── Intermediate CA
└── Other Trusted CA

For example:

SPRING_TLS_TRUSTSTORE_PASSWORD

This password protects access to the truststore.

It is unrelated to the private-key password.

