12. New Client Certificate Onboarding

If a new application needs to connect to the existing Config Server using mTLS, it needs a client certificate.

Conceptually:

Generate Private Key
        │
        ▼
Generate CSR
        │
        ▼
Submit CSR to Corporate CA
        │
        ▼
CA Issues Client Certificate
        │
        ▼
Client Certificate
+
Private Key
+
Certificate Chain
        │
        ▼
Create PKCS12 / JKS
        │
        ▼
App Client Keystore
        │
        ▼
Deploy to App Server
        │
        ▼
Spring Config Client
        │
        ▼
Config Server mTLS

The important point is that the newly requested “Config certificate” may actually mean:

Certificate used by the application
to authenticate to the Config Server

It is therefore a client certificate, not the Config Server’s own server certificate.

⸻

13. Creating a JKS from Certificate + Private Key

Suppose the certificate onboarding process provides:

client.crt
client.key
intermediate.crt

First create a PKCS#12 keystore:

openssl pkcs12 -export \
  -inkey client.key \
  -in client.crt \
  -certfile intermediate.crt \
  -name app-client \
  -out app-client.p12

Then convert it to JKS if JKS is required:

keytool -importkeystore \
  -srckeystore app-client.p12 \
  -srcstoretype PKCS12 \
  -destkeystore app-client.jks \
  -deststoretype JKS

The result should contain:

app-client.jks
│
└── PrivateKeyEntry
      ├── Client Certificate
      ├── Private Key
      └── Certificate Chain

⸻

14. Inspecting a JKS

Use:

keytool -list -v \
  -keystore webbroker.delta.td.com.jks

Important fields include:

Keystore type
Alias name
Entry type
Owner
Issuer
Serial number
Valid from
SubjectAlternativeName
ExtendedKeyUsage

For a client keystore, the important result is:

Entry type: PrivateKeyEntry

This means the entry contains a private key in addition to the certificate.

A result such as:

Entry type: trustedCertEntry

means it contains only a trusted certificate and no associated private key.

⸻

15. Inspecting a PEM Certificate

Use:

openssl x509 \
  -in wtinv-api.dev.td.com.crt.pem \
  -noout \
  -subject \
  -issuer \
  -serial \
  -dates

For more detail:

openssl x509 \
  -in wtinv-api.dev.td.com.crt.pem \
  -text \
  -noout

Useful fields include:

Subject
Issuer
Validity
Subject Alternative Name
Extended Key Usage

Extended Key Usage can help identify the intended certificate purpose.

For example:

TLS Web Server Authentication

means:

serverAuth

while:

TLS Web Client Authentication

means:

clientAuth

A certificate may also support both.

⸻

16. Quick Reference

App Server
│
├── Client Keystore (.jks)
│      │
│      ├── Client Certificate
│      └── Private Key
│
│      Purpose:
│      Prove App identity to Config Server
│
├── Truststore (cacerts)
│      │
│      └── Trusted CA Certificates
│
│      Purpose:
│      Verify Config Server certificate
│
└── API HTTPS Certificate
       │
       ├── app.crt.pem
       └── app.key.pem
       Purpose:
       Prove App identity to API consumers

The easiest way to remember the architecture is:

Keystore
   =
Who am I?
Truststore
   =
Who do I trust?
CRT
   =
Certificate / public identity
Private Key
   =
Proof that I own that identity

And for this architecture:

                Config Server Connection
App JKS ---------------------------------> Config Server
"Here is my client identity"
App cacerts <----------------------------- Config Server
"I trust the CA that issued your identity"
                    App API
API Consumer ----------------------------> App
                                           │
                                           ├── CRT.PEM
                                           └── KEY.PEM
                                           "Here is my server identity"